insert into questions (id, unit, topic, concept, stem, choices, answer, why) values
  ('P01', 'Portfolio Management', 'Portfolio Risk and Return', 'Expected return concept', 'An analyst models a stock''s return next year as one of three possible outcomes, each with a stated probability. To calculate the stock''s expected return, the analyst should:', '["Multiply each possible outcome by its probability and sum the products","Take the simple average of the three possible outcomes, ignoring their probabilities","Use only the single most likely outcome, since it has the highest probability of occurring"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Expected return is a probability-weighted average, so each outcome is multiplied by its probability of occurring and the products are summed; outcomes that are more likely to occur pull the expected value toward them. A simple average across the three outcomes is only correct in the special case where all three probabilities happen to be equal, and using only the most likely single outcome ignores that the other two outcomes still affect the return investors should expect on average.'),
  ('P02', 'Portfolio Management', 'Portfolio Risk and Return', 'Expected return, single asset', 'A biotech stock is expected to return 18% if its drug trial succeeds (probability 0.25), 9% if results are mixed (probability 0.55), and -6% if the trial fails (probability 0.20). The stock''s expected return is closest to:', '["7.00%","8.25%","10.65%"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Expected return weights each outcome by its probability: 0.25(18%) + 0.55(9%) + 0.20(-6%) = 8.25%. The 7.00% figure is the plain, unweighted average of the three outcomes, and 10.65% drops the negative sign on the trial-failure scenario, treating a -6% outcome as if it were a +6% gain.'),
  ('P03', 'Portfolio Management', 'Portfolio Risk and Return', 'Portfolio expected return', 'A portfolio allocates 40% to Fund X (expected return 11%), 35% to Fund Y (expected return 7%), and 25% to Fund Z (expected return 2%). The portfolio''s expected return is closest to:', '["6.67%","6.00%","7.35%"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Portfolio expected return is the weighted average of each holding''s expected return: 0.40(11%) + 0.35(7%) + 0.25(2%) = 7.35%. The 6.67% figure is the plain, unweighted average of the three funds'' returns, and 6.00% applies the weights in reverse order (25%/35%/40% instead of 40%/35%/25%) to the same three funds.'),
  ('P04', 'Portfolio Management', 'Portfolio Risk and Return', 'Two-asset portfolio standard deviation', 'A portfolio invests 60% in Stock A (standard deviation 25%) and 40% in Stock B (standard deviation 15%). The covariance between the returns of A and B is 0.0080. The portfolio''s standard deviation is closest to:', '["17.30%","16.74%","2.99%"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Portfolio variance is 0.60^2(0.25^2) + 0.40^2(0.15^2) + 2(0.60)(0.40)(0.0080) = 0.0225 + 0.0036 + 0.00384 = 0.02994, and taking the square root gives a standard deviation of 17.30%. The 16.74% figure omits the factor of 2 on the covariance cross-term, and 2.99% reports the portfolio variance itself (0.02994, read off as a percent) without ever taking the square root to convert it to a standard deviation.'),
  ('P05', 'Portfolio Management', 'Portfolio Risk and Return', 'Two-asset portfolio standard deviation', 'An equally weighted two-asset portfolio holds Stock C (standard deviation 30%) and Stock D (standard deviation 20%). The correlation between C and D is 0.40. The portfolio''s standard deviation is closest to:', '["48.22%","21.10%","19.62%"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Covariance must first be recovered from correlation: Cov(C,D) = 0.40 x 0.30 x 0.20 = 0.024, and portfolio variance is 0.50^2(0.30^2) + 0.50^2(0.20^2) + 2(0.50)(0.50)(0.024) = 0.0225 + 0.01 + 0.012 = 0.0445, giving a standard deviation of 21.10%. The 48.22% figure plugs the correlation coefficient 0.40 directly into the covariance term instead of first multiplying it by both standard deviations, and 19.62% uses the correctly converted covariance but omits the factor of 2 on the cross-product term.'),
  ('P06', 'Portfolio Management', 'Portfolio Risk and Return', 'Two-asset portfolio standard deviation', 'A portfolio holds 45% in Stock E (standard deviation 22%) and 55% in Stock F (standard deviation 16%), and the returns of E and F are uncorrelated. The portfolio''s standard deviation is closest to:', '["0.00%","18.94%","13.25%"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Zero correlation eliminates the covariance cross-term, but each asset''s own weighted variance still contributes: portfolio variance is 0.45^2(0.22^2) + 0.55^2(0.16^2) = 0.0098 + 0.0077 = 0.0175, giving a standard deviation of 13.25%. The 0.00% figure mistakes uncorrelated for risk-free, forgetting that each asset''s individual variance remains in the formula even when the cross-term vanishes, and 18.94% forgets to square the portfolio weights before multiplying by each variance.'),
  ('P07', 'Portfolio Management', 'Portfolio Risk and Return', 'Two-asset portfolio standard deviation', 'A portfolio holds 30% in Stock G (standard deviation 35%) and 70% in Stock H (standard deviation 18%). The correlation between G and H is 0.25. The portfolio''s standard deviation is closest to:', '["17.38%","26.37%","18.31%"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Covariance is 0.25 x 0.35 x 0.18 = 0.01575, and portfolio variance is 0.30^2(0.35^2) + 0.70^2(0.18^2) + 2(0.30)(0.70)(0.01575) = 0.011025 + 0.015876 + 0.006615 = 0.033516, giving a standard deviation of 18.31%. The 17.38% figure uses the correct covariance but forgets the factor of 2 on the cross-product term, and 26.37% pairs the 30% weight with H''s 18% standard deviation and the 70% weight with G''s 35% standard deviation, the reverse of the actual holdings.'),
  ('P08', 'Portfolio Management', 'Portfolio Risk and Return', 'Diversification effect', 'Two stocks each have a standard deviation of 20% and are combined in equal weights in a portfolio. As the correlation between the two stocks'' returns falls from 1.0 to a value below 1.0 (holding the weights and individual standard deviations fixed), the portfolio''s standard deviation:', '["Falls below the weighted average of the two stocks'' individual standard deviations","Rises above the weighted average, since two assets moving independently create more unpredictable outcomes than two assets moving together","Stays exactly at the weighted average of the two stocks'' individual standard deviations, since the weights and individual risks are unchanged"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Whenever correlation is below 1.0, the covariance cross-term in the portfolio variance formula is smaller than it would be under perfect positive correlation, so some of each asset''s individual risk is diversified away and portfolio standard deviation drops below the simple weighted average of the two individual standard deviations. The independent-assets-are-riskier claim has the direction backwards: independence is exactly what allows a loss in one asset to be offset by a gain in the other, reducing rather than increasing portfolio-level uncertainty, and the stay-the-same answer describes only the special case of correlation equal to exactly 1.0, where diversification provides no risk reduction at all.'),
  ('P09', 'Portfolio Management', 'Portfolio Risk and Return', 'Correlation from covariance', 'The covariance between the returns of Stock I and Stock J is 0.0195. Stock I''s standard deviation is 26% and Stock J''s is 15%. The correlation between the two stocks'' returns is closest to:', '["0.13","0.50","0.05"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Correlation equals covariance divided by the product of the two standard deviations: 0.0195 / (0.26 x 0.15) = 0.0195 / 0.039 = 0.50. The 0.13 figure divides the covariance by Stock J''s standard deviation alone, forgetting that the denominator needs both standard deviations multiplied together, and 0.05 divides by the sum of the two standard deviations (0.26 + 0.15 = 0.41) rather than their product.'),
  ('P10', 'Portfolio Management', 'Portfolio Risk and Return', 'Correlation and diversification', 'Stocks K and L each have a standard deviation of 20% and are held in an equally weighted two-stock portfolio. At a correlation of 1.0, the portfolio''s standard deviation equals 20.00%, the same as each stock held alone. If the correlation between K and L instead falls to 0.20 (holding the weights and individual standard deviations fixed), the portfolio''s standard deviation is closest to:', '["20.00%","14.83%","15.49%"]'::jsonb, 2, 'At correlation 0.20, covariance is 0.20 x 0.20 x 0.20 = 0.008, and portfolio variance is 0.50^2(0.20^2) + 0.50^2(0.20^2) + 2(0.50)(0.50)(0.008) = 0.01 + 0.01 + 0.004 = 0.024, giving a standard deviation of 15.49%, below the 20.00% figure that would apply only if correlation stayed at 1.0. The 20.00% choice wrongly assumes correlation has no effect on portfolio risk as long as weights and individual standard deviations are unchanged, and 14.83% redoes the calculation at the new 0.20 correlation but forgets the factor of 2 on the covariance cross-term.'),
  ('P11', 'Portfolio Management', 'Portfolio Management Process', 'Three-step process overview', 'The portfolio management process is best described as consisting of which three sequential steps?', '["Diversification, monitoring, and liquidation","Planning, execution, and feedback","Budgeting, forecasting, and auditing"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The portfolio management process is a continuous cycle of planning (establishing objectives, constraints, and capital market expectations), execution (translating the plan into an actual asset allocation and portfolio of securities), and feedback (monitoring the portfolio and rebalancing as needed). Diversification, monitoring, and liquidation names some real activities that occur inside the process but not the three formal named steps, and budgeting, forecasting, and auditing borrows corporate-finance and accounting terminology that does not describe this specific investment framework.'),
  ('P12', 'Portfolio Management', 'Portfolio Management Process', 'Planning step', 'During the planning step of the portfolio management process, the portfolio manager primarily focuses on:', '["Identifying the client''s objectives and constraints and forming capital market expectations","Selecting individual securities and executing trades to build the portfolio","Comparing realized portfolio performance against a benchmark"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Planning is where the manager identifies the client''s return objectives, risk tolerance, and constraints and combines them with capital market expectations to set the strategy''s direction, before any actual portfolio is built. Selecting securities and executing trades belongs to the execution step, which follows planning, and comparing performance against a benchmark belongs to the feedback step, which follows execution.'),
  ('P13', 'Portfolio Management', 'Portfolio Management Process', 'Execution step', 'The execution step of the portfolio management process most directly involves:', '["Drafting the client''s written statement of investment objectives and constraints","Evaluating whether the portfolio met its stated objectives over the period","Implementing the strategy through asset allocation decisions and the selection of specific securities"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Execution takes the strategy set during planning and puts it into practice: deciding how to allocate capital across asset classes and choosing the specific securities that fill each allocation. Drafting the objectives and constraints document happens earlier, during planning, and evaluating whether objectives were met happens later, during the feedback step.'),
  ('P14', 'Portfolio Management', 'Portfolio Management Process', 'Feedback step', 'The feedback step of the portfolio management process is best described as:', '["Setting the client''s initial return objectives and risk tolerance","Executing the trades needed to establish the portfolio''s initial asset allocation","Monitoring the portfolio and the client''s circumstances, then rebalancing or updating the strategy as needed"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Feedback is the ongoing monitoring of portfolio performance, the markets, and the client''s own circumstances, with rebalancing or a revised strategy following whenever something has drifted from plan. Setting initial objectives and risk tolerance is part of planning, and executing the trades that establish the initial allocation is part of execution -- both earlier stages that feedback loops back to influence.'),
  ('P15', 'Portfolio Management', 'Portfolio Management Process', 'Identifying the process step', 'A portfolio manager notices that a client''s equity allocation has drifted five percentage points above its target weight after a strong market rally, and sells equities to bring the portfolio back in line with the original targets. This activity belongs to which step of the portfolio management process?', '["Execution, since it involves placing trades in the market","Feedback, since it involves monitoring drift and realigning the portfolio","Planning, since it involves the portfolio''s target weights"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Monitoring the portfolio for drift away from its target weights and rebalancing back toward them is the defining activity of the feedback step, even though the actual rebalancing trades are placed in the market. Execution is tempting because trades are indeed involved, but execution refers to the initial implementation of a newly set strategy, not the ongoing monitoring-and-correction cycle that triggered these particular trades, and planning refers to setting those target weights in the first place, not the later act of realigning to them.'),
  ('P16', 'Portfolio Management', 'Portfolio Management Process', 'Iterative process', 'A client''s financial circumstances change substantially after a divorce, materially altering her liquidity needs and risk tolerance. The portfolio manager updates the client''s investment policy statement and revises the strategic asset allocation as a result. This best illustrates that the portfolio management process is:', '["A dynamic, iterative cycle in which a change in the client''s circumstances can restart the process back at planning","A strictly linear, one-time sequence that is not revisited once the initial plan is implemented","Primarily driven by capital market expectations rather than by the client''s personal circumstances"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Because a major life change flows all the way back into a revised investment policy statement and a new strategic asset allocation, this shows the process looping back to its planning step rather than running once and ending; feedback about the client''s changed situation becomes new input to planning. The linear, one-time-only description is exactly what this scenario contradicts, and while capital market expectations are also a planning input, this scenario is driven by a personal life event, not a market view.'),
  ('P17', 'Portfolio Management', 'Portfolio Planning and Construction', 'IPS purpose', 'The primary purpose of an investment policy statement (IPS) is to:', '["Guarantee a minimum investment return regardless of market conditions","Recommend the specific securities the manager should purchase immediately","Document the client''s objectives and constraints so the portfolio can be managed consistently against them"]'::jsonb, 2, 'An IPS is the governing document that captures a client''s return objectives, risk tolerance, and constraints (liquidity, time horizon, taxes, legal and regulatory factors, and unique circumstances), giving the manager a stable reference point for every future decision. It does not promise any particular return outcome, since markets are uncertain, and it does not itself name specific securities, which are chosen later during execution based on the strategy the IPS supports.'),
  ('P18', 'Portfolio Management', 'Portfolio Planning and Construction', 'IPS objectives vs constraints', 'Within an investment policy statement, an investor''s desired risk and return targets are classified as:', '["Constraints, distinct from objectives such as liquidity needs and time horizon","Objectives, distinct from constraints such as liquidity needs, time horizon, and tax status","Neither objectives nor constraints, since risk and return targets are set by the manager, not the client"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Risk and return targets are the investor''s objectives -- what the portfolio is trying to achieve -- while liquidity needs, time horizon, tax status, legal and regulatory factors, and unique circumstances are constraints, the limitations within which those objectives must be pursued. Risk and return targets are always specific to the individual client''s own goals and circumstances, not a generic figure set unilaterally by the manager.'),
  ('P19', 'Portfolio Management', 'Portfolio Planning and Construction', 'Strategic asset allocation', 'Strategic asset allocation refers to:', '["The set of long-term target weights across asset classes derived from the client''s IPS and long-run capital market expectations","Short-term deviations from target weights taken to exploit a perceived temporary mispricing","The day-to-day selection of individual securities within a single asset class"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Strategic asset allocation sets the long-run policy weights across asset classes that are expected to satisfy the client''s objectives and constraints over time, anchored to the IPS and to long-term capital market expectations. Short-term, opportunistic deviations from those target weights describe tactical asset allocation instead, and choosing individual securities within an asset class is a separate, later decision that happens once the allocation across asset classes is already set.'),
  ('P20', 'Portfolio Management', 'Portfolio Planning and Construction', 'Strategic vs tactical allocation', 'A portfolio''s long-term policy weight to equities is 60%, but the manager temporarily increases the allocation to 65% because she believes equities are undervalued over the next quarter. This temporary shift is an example of:', '["Strategic asset allocation","Tactical asset allocation","A change to the investment policy statement''s stated objectives"]'::jsonb, 1, 'A short-term, opportunistic deviation from the long-run policy weights, motivated by a near-term market view, is tactical asset allocation. Strategic asset allocation refers to the long-run 60% target weight itself, not this temporary departure from it, and the client''s stated objectives in the IPS have not changed at all -- only the manager''s short-term positioning around the unchanged long-term target has.'),
  ('P21', 'Portfolio Management', 'Portfolio Planning and Construction', 'Ability vs willingness to take risk', 'An investor has a stable, high income, a long time horizon, and no near-term liquidity needs, giving her a high ability to take risk. However, she reports significant anxiety about short-term portfolio losses and insists on a conservative allocation. The investment policy statement should most appropriately reflect:', '["An aggressive risk tolerance, since her high ability to take risk should override her stated anxiety about losses","A moderate-to-conservative risk tolerance, since a below-average willingness to take risk should generally temper a high ability to take risk","An aggressive risk tolerance, since ability and willingness to take risk always move together for the same investor"]'::jsonb, 1, 'When ability and willingness to take risk conflict, the more conservative of the two is generally given greater weight in setting the IPS''s risk tolerance, since taking on risk the client cannot psychologically tolerate can lead to poorly timed selling during downturns. Overriding her stated anxiety in favor of pure financial capacity ignores that willingness matters too, and assuming the two always move together contradicts the very scenario described, where a high ability coexists with a low willingness.'),
  ('P22', 'Portfolio Management', 'Portfolio Planning and Construction', 'IPS objective vs constraint', 'A client tells her advisor that she must keep $50,000 in cash available at all times to cover a planned home renovation beginning in 18 months. Within her investment policy statement, this requirement is best classified as:', '["A return objective","A risk objective","A liquidity constraint"]'::jsonb, 2, 'A specific, near-term cash need that limits how the portfolio can be invested is a liquidity constraint, one of the standard constraint categories alongside time horizon, taxes, legal and regulatory factors, and unique circumstances. It is not a return objective, which describes the growth or income the portfolio is meant to generate, and it is not a risk objective, which describes how much volatility or potential loss the client is willing to accept -- this requirement instead limits which assets are even eligible to hold the $50,000, regardless of return or risk preferences.'),
  ('P23', 'Portfolio Management', 'Risk Aversion and Utility Theory', 'Risk-averse investor', 'An investor is offered a choice between a certain payment of $500 and a gamble with an expected value of exactly $500 (a 50% chance of $1,000 and a 50% chance of $0). A risk-averse investor will:', '["Prefer the certain $500 payment over the gamble, even though both have the same expected value","Prefer the gamble over the certain $500 payment, since the gamble offers a chance at a larger payoff","Be indifferent between the certain payment and the gamble, since both have the same expected value"]'::jsonb, 0, 'A risk-averse investor derives less additional satisfaction from an extra dollar of gain than the satisfaction lost from an equivalent dollar of loss, so when expected value is equal, the certain outcome is preferred over the gamble''s uncertainty. Preferring the gamble for its upside describes risk-seeking behavior, and being indifferent between the two describes a risk-neutral investor, who cares only about expected value and assigns no penalty to risk itself.'),
  ('P24', 'Portfolio Management', 'Risk Aversion and Utility Theory', 'Risk-neutral vs risk-seeking', 'An investor who evaluates choices using only their expected value, with no adjustment for risk, and an investor who will accept a lower expected value purely for a chance at a large payoff, are best described respectively as:', '["Risk-averse and risk-neutral","Risk-seeking and risk-averse","Risk-neutral and risk-seeking"]'::jsonb, 2, 'An investor who judges alternatives solely on expected value, indifferent to the risk surrounding it, is risk-neutral, while an investor willing to accept a lower expected value in exchange for a shot at a large payoff, as in lottery-ticket-like behavior, is risk-seeking. Risk-averse describes neither investor here, since a risk-averse investor requires compensation, in the form of a higher expected return, for bearing additional risk, rather than being indifferent to risk or actively seeking it out.'),
  ('P25', 'Portfolio Management', 'Risk Aversion and Utility Theory', 'Utility function calculation', 'Using the utility function U = E(R) - 0.5 x A x (variance), where E(R) and the variance are both expressed in decimal form, an investor with a risk aversion coefficient A of 3 evaluates a portfolio with an expected return of 14% and a standard deviation of 20%. This investor''s utility for the portfolio is closest to:', '["-16.00%","8.00%","2.00%"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Converting to decimals, variance is 0.20^2 = 0.04, so U = 0.14 - 0.5(3)(0.04) = 0.14 - 0.06 = 0.08, or 8.00%. The -16.00% figure mistakenly uses the standard deviation itself (0.20) in place of the variance (0.04), and 2.00% correctly squares the standard deviation but forgets to apply the 0.5 coefficient in front of A, computing 0.14 - 3(0.04) instead. This bank consistently uses the CFA curriculum''s decimal-input convention (E(R) and variance as decimals, 0.5 coefficient on A); a common alternate form scales E(R) and standard deviation as whole percentage-point numbers with a rescaled 0.005 coefficient (U = E(R) - 0.005 x A x variance, variance in percentage-points-squared) -- mathematically equivalent, but mixing decimal inputs with the 0.005 coefficient (or percentage-point inputs with 0.5) understates or overstates utility by a factor of 100, so always confirm which convention a source uses before plugging in numbers.'),
  ('P26', 'Portfolio Management', 'Risk Aversion and Utility Theory', 'Utility function calculation', 'Using the utility function U = E(R) - 0.5 x A x (variance), where E(R) and the variance are both expressed in decimal form, a portfolio with an expected return of 20% and a standard deviation of 20% delivers a utility of 8% to a particular investor. This investor''s risk aversion coefficient, A, is closest to:', '["1.20","3.00","6.00"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Rearranging the utility formula gives A = (E(R) - U) / (0.5 x variance) = (0.20 - 0.08) / (0.5 x 0.20^2) = 0.12 / 0.02 = 6.00. The 1.20 figure uses the standard deviation (0.20) rather than the variance (0.04) in the denominator, and 3.00 correctly squares the standard deviation but omits the 0.5 coefficient, dividing by 0.04 instead of 0.02. This bank consistently uses the CFA curriculum''s decimal-input convention (E(R) and variance as decimals, 0.5 coefficient on A); a common alternate form scales E(R) and standard deviation as whole percentage-point numbers with a rescaled 0.005 coefficient (U = E(R) - 0.005 x A x variance, variance in percentage-points-squared) -- mathematically equivalent, but mixing decimal inputs with the 0.005 coefficient (or percentage-point inputs with 0.5) understates or overstates utility by a factor of 100, so always confirm which convention a source uses before plugging in numbers.'),
  ('P27', 'Portfolio Management', 'Risk Aversion and Utility Theory', 'Indifference curve steepness', 'In mean-variance space, Investor M''s indifference curves are noticeably steeper than Investor N''s. This most likely indicates that, relative to Investor N, Investor M:', '["Is more risk-averse and requires a larger increase in expected return to accept a given increase in risk","Is less risk-averse and requires a smaller increase in expected return to accept a given increase in risk","Holds an identical portfolio to Investor N, since indifference curve slope does not depend on risk preferences"]'::jsonb, 0, 'A steeper indifference curve means that, for a given increase in risk (standard deviation) along the horizontal axis, an investor demands a larger increase in expected return along the vertical axis to remain equally satisfied -- exactly the signature of greater risk aversion. The less-risk-averse description reverses this relationship, and indifference curve slope is in fact one of the most direct graphical expressions of an investor''s risk aversion, not something unrelated to it.'),
  ('P28', 'Portfolio Management', 'Risk Aversion and Utility Theory', 'Utility comparison across portfolios', 'Using the utility function U = E(R) - 0.5 x A x (variance), where E(R) and the variance are both expressed in decimal form, a risk-averse investor with a risk aversion coefficient A of 4 is comparing two portfolios. Portfolio 1 has an expected return of 16% and a standard deviation of 24%. Portfolio 2 has an expected return of 12% and a standard deviation of 10%. Based on utility, this investor should prefer:', '["Portfolio 1, since its expected return of 16% is higher than Portfolio 2''s 12%","Portfolio 2, since its utility of 10.00% exceeds Portfolio 1''s utility of 4.48%","Portfolio 1, since its utility of 4.48% exceeds Portfolio 2''s utility of -8.00%"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Portfolio 1''s utility is 0.16 - 0.5(4)(0.24^2) = 0.16 - 0.1152 = 0.0448, or 4.48%, while Portfolio 2''s utility is 0.12 - 0.5(4)(0.10^2) = 0.12 - 0.02 = 0.10, or 10.00%, so the lower-return but much-lower-risk Portfolio 2 delivers the higher utility once risk aversion is applied. Choosing Portfolio 1 for its higher raw expected return ignores risk entirely, exactly the comparison a risk-averse investor should not make, and the -8.00% figure for Portfolio 2 results from mistakenly using its standard deviation (0.10) rather than its variance (0.01) in the formula, understating its true utility and flipping the apparent conclusion. This bank consistently uses the CFA curriculum''s decimal-input convention (E(R) and variance as decimals, 0.5 coefficient on A); a common alternate form scales E(R) and standard deviation as whole percentage-point numbers with a rescaled 0.005 coefficient (U = E(R) - 0.005 x A x variance, variance in percentage-points-squared) -- mathematically equivalent, but mixing decimal inputs with the 0.005 coefficient (or percentage-point inputs with 0.5) understates or overstates utility by a factor of 100, so always confirm which convention a source uses before plugging in numbers.'),
  ('P29', 'Portfolio Management', 'Capital Allocation Line', 'CAL definition', 'The capital allocation line (CAL) represents:', '["The set of all possible combinations of two or more risky assets, excluding the risk-free asset entirely","The single portfolio of risky assets that has the lowest possible variance among all risky-asset combinations","The set of complete portfolios formed by combining a risk-free asset with a single risky portfolio in varying proportions"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The CAL plots every possible complete portfolio that mixes a risk-free asset with one specific risky portfolio, tracing a straight line in expected-return-versus-standard-deviation space as the proportion allocated to each is varied. A combination of only risky assets with no risk-free asset describes the risky-asset opportunity set instead, and the minimum-variance portfolio is just one single point on that opportunity set, not the entire line formed by adding the risk-free asset.'),
  ('P30', 'Portfolio Management', 'Capital Allocation Line', 'Sharpe ratio / CAL slope', 'A risky portfolio has an expected return of 13% and a standard deviation of 18%. The risk-free rate is 4%. The slope of the capital allocation line formed with this risky portfolio is closest to:', '["0.72","0.50","2.00"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The CAL''s slope is the portfolio''s Sharpe ratio: (13% - 4%) / 18% = 9% / 18% = 0.50. The 0.72 figure divides the risky portfolio''s return by its standard deviation without ever subtracting the risk-free rate first, and 2.00 inverts the ratio, dividing standard deviation by the excess return instead of the other way around.'),
  ('P31', 'Portfolio Management', 'Capital Allocation Line', 'Complete portfolio expected return', 'An investor allocates 30% of a complete portfolio to a risk-free asset yielding 3% and 70% to a risky portfolio with an expected return of 11%. The expected return of the complete portfolio is closest to:', '["7.00%","5.40%","8.60%"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The complete portfolio''s expected return is the weighted average of the risk-free rate and the risky portfolio''s expected return: 0.30(3%) + 0.70(11%) = 8.60%. The 7.00% figure is the plain, unweighted average of the two returns, and 5.40% applies the weights in reverse (70% to the risk-free asset and 30% to the risky portfolio instead of the other way around).'),
  ('P32', 'Portfolio Management', 'Capital Allocation Line', 'Complete portfolio standard deviation', 'An investor places 65% of a complete portfolio in a risky portfolio with a standard deviation of 28%, and the remainder in a risk-free asset. The standard deviation of the complete portfolio is closest to:', '["18.20%","28.00%","3.31%"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Because the risk-free asset has zero standard deviation and zero covariance with the risky portfolio, the complete portfolio''s standard deviation is simply the risky-asset weight times the risky portfolio''s own standard deviation: 0.65 x 28% = 18.20%. The 28.00% figure ignores the weighting entirely and reports the risky portfolio''s own standard deviation as if the whole portfolio were invested in it, and 3.31% computes the squared term 0.65^2 x 0.28^2 = 0.0331 correctly but reports it directly as a percent instead of recognizing it is a variance that still needs a square root.'),
  ('P33', 'Portfolio Management', 'Capital Allocation Line', 'Solving for portfolio weight', 'A risk-free asset yields 5% and a risky portfolio has an expected return of 15%. An investor wants a complete portfolio with an expected return of exactly 12%. The weight that should be placed in the risky portfolio is closest to:', '["80.00%","70.00%","30.00%"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Solving the complete-portfolio return equation for the risky weight gives w = (target - risk-free rate) / (risky return - risk-free rate) = (12% - 5%) / (15% - 5%) = 7% / 10% = 70.00%. The 80.00% figure divides the target return directly by the risky portfolio''s return (12%/15%) as though the risk-free rate were zero, and 30.00% correctly finds the complementary weight left over for the risk-free asset but reports that figure as the weight in the risky portfolio instead.'),
  ('P34', 'Portfolio Management', 'Capital Allocation Line', 'Weight and return from target risk', 'A risky portfolio has a standard deviation of 30% and an expected return of 16%. The risk-free rate is 4%. An investor wants a complete portfolio with a standard deviation of exactly 21%. The expected return of this complete portfolio is closest to:', '["70.00%","7.60%","12.40%"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The weight needed in the risky portfolio is 21% / 30% = 70%, so the complete portfolio''s expected return is 0.30(4%) + 0.70(16%) = 1.2% + 11.2% = 12.40%. The 70.00% figure stops after finding the required weight and never takes the second step of computing the resulting expected return, and 7.60% swaps the roles of the two weights, applying 70% to the risk-free asset and 30% to the risky portfolio instead of the reverse.'),
  ('P35', 'Portfolio Management', 'Capital Allocation Line', 'Lending vs borrowing portfolios', 'An investor places 130% of a complete portfolio''s value in a risky portfolio, funding the extra 30% by borrowing at the risk-free rate. Relative to a complete portfolio that places only 70% in the same risky portfolio and the rest in the risk-free asset, this borrowing portfolio:', '["Lies further out along the same capital allocation line, with higher expected return and higher risk","Lies on a different, flatter capital allocation line, since borrowing changes the line''s slope","Lies closer to the risk-free asset on the same capital allocation line, since borrowing reduces net risk exposure"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Borrowing at the risk-free rate to invest more than 100% of the portfolio''s value in the risky portfolio moves the investor further out along the very same capital allocation line, beyond the risky portfolio''s own point, delivering higher expected return along with higher risk in exchange for the leverage. The CAL''s slope, the Sharpe ratio, is a property of the risky portfolio and the risk-free rate alone and does not change merely because an investor chooses to lend or borrow, and borrowing increases net risk exposure by adding leverage, rather than reducing it.'),
  ('P36', 'Portfolio Management', 'Capital Allocation Line', 'Leveraged complete portfolio', 'An investor borrows at the risk-free rate of 6% to invest 130% of a complete portfolio''s value in a risky portfolio with an expected return of 14%, funding the extra 30% with the borrowed money. The expected return of this leveraged complete portfolio is closest to:', '["20.00%","16.40%","14.00%"]'::jsonb, 1, 'With 130% in the risky portfolio, the weight on the risk-free asset is -30% (borrowed, not invested), so the complete portfolio''s expected return is (-0.30)(6%) + 1.30(14%) = -1.8% + 18.2% = 16.40%. The 20.00% figure incorrectly treats the borrowed 30% as a positive investment in the risk-free asset rather than a negative one, adding its return instead of subtracting the cost of borrowing it, and 14.00% simply reports the unleveraged risky portfolio''s own return, ignoring that borrowed money is being put to work as well.'),
  ('P37', 'Portfolio Management', 'Capital Market Theory', 'CAL vs CML', 'The capital market line (CML) differs from a generic capital allocation line (CAL) in that the CML:', '["Specifically uses the market portfolio as its risky portfolio, and applies only to fully diversified, efficient portfolios","Uses total risk (standard deviation) instead of beta, while a generic CAL always uses beta","Applies to individual securities as easily as it applies to portfolios, unlike a generic CAL"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The CML is the specific CAL obtained when the risky portfolio is the market portfolio itself, so it applies only to portfolios that are fully diversified and efficient; any other risky portfolio paired with the risk-free asset produces a different, generic CAL. Both the CML and a generic CAL use standard deviation (total risk) on the horizontal axis, not beta, and the CML does not usefully apply to individual securities or inefficient portfolios, since their total risk includes diversifiable risk that the market does not compensate.'),
  ('P38', 'Portfolio Management', 'Capital Market Theory', 'Systematic vs unsystematic risk', 'Unsystematic risk is best described as risk that:', '["Cannot be eliminated through diversification and is the only risk priced by the market","Affects every security in the market simultaneously and cannot be avoided by any investor","Can be eliminated through diversification and is not rewarded with additional expected return"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Unsystematic (firm-specific) risk can be diversified away by holding a broad enough portfolio, and because it can be eliminated at no cost, the market does not reward investors with extra expected return for bearing it. Systematic (market) risk is the risk that cannot be diversified away, affects all securities to varying degrees, and is the only risk that is priced -- exactly the description that the two wrong choices apply to unsystematic risk instead.'),
  ('P39', 'Portfolio Management', 'Capital Market Theory', 'Beta from covariance', 'The covariance between a stock''s returns and the market''s returns is 0.0270. The variance of the market''s returns is 0.0225. The stock''s beta is closest to:', '["0.83","1.20","0.18"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Beta equals the covariance between the stock and the market divided by the variance of the market: 0.0270 / 0.0225 = 1.20. The 0.83 figure inverts the ratio (market variance over covariance instead of covariance over market variance), and 0.18 divides the covariance by the market''s standard deviation (0.15, the square root of 0.0225) rather than by its variance.'),
  ('P40', 'Portfolio Management', 'Capital Market Theory', 'Beta from correlation and standard deviations', 'A stock''s returns have a correlation of 0.60 with the market''s returns. The stock''s standard deviation is 27% and the market''s standard deviation is 18%. The stock''s beta is closest to:', '["0.40","0.54","0.90"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Beta equals correlation times the ratio of the stock''s own standard deviation to the market''s: 0.60 x (27% / 18%) = 0.60 x 1.5 = 0.90. The 0.40 figure inverts the standard deviation ratio (market over stock instead of stock over market), and 0.54 uses the correlation squared, 0.36 (which is R-squared, not correlation), in place of the correlation coefficient itself.'),
  ('P41', 'Portfolio Management', 'Capital Market Theory', 'CAPM required return', 'The risk-free rate is 4% and the expected return on the market is 11%. A stock has a beta of 1.4. Using the CAPM, this stock''s required return is closest to:', '["13.80%","19.40%","9.80%"]'::jsonb, 0, 'CAPM required return is the risk-free rate plus beta times the market risk premium: 4% + 1.4(11% - 4%) = 4% + 9.8% = 13.80%. The 19.40% figure multiplies beta by the market return alone without ever subtracting the risk-free rate first (4% + 1.4 x 11%), the single most common CAPM error, and 9.80% correctly computes beta times the market risk premium but forgets to add the risk-free rate back at the end.'),
  ('P42', 'Portfolio Management', 'Capital Market Theory', 'SML vs CML', 'Unlike the capital market line, the security market line (SML):', '["Plots required return against beta (systematic risk), and applies to any individual security or portfolio, efficient or not","Plots required return against standard deviation (total risk), and applies only to efficient portfolios","Is used only to price risk-free assets, since all risky securities are priced using the CML instead"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The SML plots required return against beta, so it can price any individual asset or portfolio, efficient or not, since beta only reflects systematic risk regardless of how much diversifiable risk an asset also carries. Plotting return against standard deviation, restricted to efficient portfolios, describes the CML instead, and the SML is precisely the tool used to price risky securities, not risk-free ones, whose required return is simply the risk-free rate by definition.'),
  ('P43', 'Portfolio Management', 'Capital Market Theory', 'SML over/undervalued asset', 'The risk-free rate is 3% and the expected return on the market is 10%. Stock N has a beta of 1.2, and an analyst forecasts that Stock N will return 11.5% over the next year. Based on the CAPM and the security market line, Stock N is most likely:', '["Undervalued, since the forecast return of 11.5% exceeds the CAPM-required return of 11.4%, placing it above the SML","Overvalued, since the forecast return of 11.5% exceeds the CAPM-required return of 11.4%","Overvalued, since the forecast return of 11.5% falls short of a required return of 15.0%"]'::jsonb, 0, 'CAPM required return is 3% + 1.2(10% - 3%) = 3% + 8.4% = 11.4%, and because the analyst''s 11.5% forecast exceeds this required return, Stock N plots above the SML and is undervalued, offering more return than its systematic risk alone would demand. The second choice performs the same correct arithmetic but inverts the labeling logic, since a forecast return above the SML always signals undervaluation, never overvaluation, and the third choice arrives at a required return of 15.0% only by multiplying beta by the market return alone (3% + 1.2 x 10%) without first subtracting the risk-free rate, the same CAPM shortcut error tested elsewhere in this bank.'),
  ('P44', 'Portfolio Management', 'Capital Market Theory', 'CAPM algebra', 'The risk-free rate is 5% and the expected return on the market is 12%. A stock''s CAPM-required return is 19%. This stock''s beta must be closest to:', '["2.71","2.00","1.17"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Rearranging the CAPM formula for beta gives beta = (required return - risk-free rate) / (market return - risk-free rate) = (19% - 5%) / (12% - 5%) = 14% / 7% = 2.00. The 2.71 figure divides the full 19% required return by the 7% market risk premium without first netting out the risk-free rate from the numerator, and 1.17 divides the correctly netted 14% numerator by the market return alone (12%) instead of by the market risk premium.'),
  ('P45', 'Portfolio Management', 'Behavioral Finance', 'Overconfidence bias', 'An investor believes his stock-picking skill is well above average, trades far more frequently than his peers as a result, and is surprised to learn his actual returns lag a simple index fund. This behavior most directly illustrates:', '["Overconfidence bias","Loss aversion","Mental accounting"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Overestimating one''s own knowledge or ability, and trading excessively as a result, is the signature pattern of overconfidence bias, which research repeatedly links to underperformance driven by the transaction costs and timing errors that excessive trading creates. Loss aversion describes feeling losses more painfully than equivalent gains rather than overrating one''s own skill, and mental accounting describes treating different pools of money differently rather than a belief about one''s own ability.'),
  ('P46', 'Portfolio Management', 'Behavioral Finance', 'Loss aversion', 'An investor holds a stock that has fallen 20% below its purchase price and refuses to sell it, saying she will wait until it at least gets back to what she paid. The same investor readily sells a different stock that has risen 20% above its purchase price. This pattern of behavior is best explained by:', '["Risk aversion, since she is simply avoiding any position with negative expected future returns","Loss aversion, since the pain of realizing a loss looms larger to her than the pleasure of realizing an equivalent gain","Confirmation bias, since she is seeking information that supports her original purchase decision"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Anchoring the decision to sell on the original purchase price, and being unwilling to realize a loss while being quick to realize a similar-sized gain, is the disposition effect that loss aversion produces: losses feel more painful than equivalent gains feel pleasurable, distorting the sell decision away from the stock''s actual future prospects. This is not simple risk aversion, which is a rational preference for less volatility at a given expected return and would not by itself explain holding one loser while selling a comparable winner, and there is no evidence here of selectively seeking out confirming information, only a reluctance tied to the original purchase price.'),
  ('P47', 'Portfolio Management', 'Behavioral Finance', 'Anchoring and adjustment', 'An analyst''s initial fair value estimate for a stock was $50 per share. After receiving clearly positive new earnings guidance that, on its own merits, would justify a fair value near $65, the analyst revises the estimate to only $53. This best illustrates:', '["Anchoring and adjustment, since the revision stayed too close to the original $50 estimate despite strongly positive new information","Herding, since the analyst is following the consensus view of other analysts","Mental accounting, since the analyst is treating the earnings guidance as a separate account from the stock''s price"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Fixating on an initial reference point, here the original $50 estimate, and then adjusting insufficiently even when new information clearly justifies a much larger revision, is the definition of anchoring and adjustment. Herding involves following the actions or views of a crowd rather than one''s own earlier estimate, which is not described here, and mental accounting involves segregating money into separate mental buckets rather than under-adjusting a single valuation estimate.'),
  ('P48', 'Portfolio Management', 'Behavioral Finance', 'Mental accounting', 'An investor keeps a safe retirement account invested entirely in bonds and a separate speculative account, funded by an inheritance, invested entirely in highly volatile small-cap stocks, without ever considering the combined risk of both accounts together as a single portfolio. This best illustrates:', '["Overconfidence, since the investor believes his stock-picking skill justifies the speculative account","Anchoring and adjustment, since the investor is anchored to the inheritance''s original dollar amount","Mental accounting, since money is being treated differently according to which mental bucket it came from, rather than as part of one combined portfolio"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Treating the retirement money and the inheritance money as belonging to separate mental compartments, each with its own risk rules, rather than evaluating the risk of the combined portfolio as a whole, is the defining pattern of mental accounting. Nothing in the scenario points to a belief about the investor''s own stock-picking ability, which would be overconfidence, and there is no described fixation on the inheritance''s original dollar figure when making later decisions, which would be needed for anchoring and adjustment.'),
  ('P49', 'Portfolio Management', 'Behavioral Finance', 'Herding', 'An investor buys shares of a company purely because it has been the most talked-about stock on financial social media for several weeks and many other retail investors are buying it, without forming any independent view of the company''s fundamentals. This behavior is best described as:', '["Herding","Confirmation bias","Loss aversion"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Buying a security primarily because a large group of other investors is buying it, rather than because of one''s own independent analysis, is herding. Confirmation bias involves seeking out or overweighting information that supports a belief the investor already holds, which requires an existing view that is absent here, and loss aversion concerns the asymmetric pain of realized losses versus gains, not the decision to follow the crowd into a new position.'),
  ('P50', 'Portfolio Management', 'Behavioral Finance', 'Confirmation bias', 'After forming the view that a company is a strong buy, an investor actively seeks out analyst reports and news articles that support a bullish outlook, while dismissing a detailed short-seller report that raises specific accounting concerns as not credible without examining its evidence. This behavior is best described as:', '["Overconfidence, since the investor is certain his original analysis was correct","Confirmation bias, since the investor is selectively seeking evidence that supports the existing bullish view while dismissing contrary evidence without examination","Herding, since the investor is following the views of other bullish analysts"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Actively seeking out supportive information while dismissing specific, detailed contrary evidence without genuinely engaging with it is the hallmark of confirmation bias -- the existing belief shapes which evidence gets taken seriously rather than the evidence shaping the belief. Overconfidence is a tempting label since unwarranted certainty is present, but overconfidence describes overestimating one''s own ability or the precision of one''s own judgment in general, while this scenario specifically describes a lopsided pattern of seeking confirming evidence and dismissing disconfirming evidence; there is also no indication the investor is simply following other analysts'' bullish views, which would be needed for herding.');
